  ("•  La clienta ‘female customer’ is increasingly accepted, at least in Spain, but la cliente is also" 0.1092 0.8945 0.5761 0.5926 0.5926 0.0165 0.00482 0.3373 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("heard." 0.1305 0.1844 0.5926 0.6091 0.6091 0.0165 nil 0.1620 0.0539 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("•  El/la jefe: la jefa is accepted by El País as the feminine of el/la jefe ‘boss’, but it sounds too" 0.1092 0.8763 0.6091 0.6256 0.6256 0.0165 0.00482 0.3430 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("familiar for some people. García Márquez (Col.) writes Maruja había sido . . . jefe de relaciones" 0.1305 0.9039 0.6256 0.6421 0.6421 0.0165 0.00482 0.3932 0.0671 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("públicas ‘Maruja had been head of public relations’." 0.1305 0.5634 0.6421 0.6586 0.6586 0.0165 0.00482 0.3350 0.0639 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("•  El/la juez ‘judge’ – the preferred form in Spain, Mexico and Peru: El País insists on la juez." 0.1092 0.8902 0.6586 0.6751 0.6751 0.0165 0.00482 0.3612 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("Elsewhere in Latin America la jueza is not uncommon for a female judge. The Academy" 0.1305 0.8702 0.6751 0.6915 0.6915 0.0165 0.00482 0.3855 0.0878 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("accepts la jueza and it is widespread in speech everywhere." 0.1305 0.6292 0.6916 0.7080 0.7080 0.0165 0.00482 0.3156 0.0620 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("•  El médico ‘doctor’: la médica is normal in much of Latin America, cf. una médica blanca" 0.1092 0.8625 0.7081 0.7246 0.7246 0.0165 0.00482 0.3854 0.0166 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("sudafricana (Granma, Cu.) ‘a white South-African female doctor’, but Emilia Saura, la" 0.1307 0.8460 0.7246 0.7410 0.7410 0.0165 0.00482 0.3615 0.0912 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("médico sin hospital (AM, Mex.) ‘ES, the doctor without a hospital’. El País and the Academy" 0.1307 0.9059 0.7411 0.7575 0.7575 0.0165 0.00482 0.3942 0.0565 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("approve of la médica and it is gaining ground though some people still considerate it" 0.1307 0.8527 0.7576 0.7740 0.7740 0.0165 0.00482 0.3246 0.0712 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("slightly disrespectful. La doctora is polite alternative for a woman doctor. The Academy" 0.1307 0.8757 0.7741 0.7905 0.7905 0.0165 0.00482 0.3408 0.0646 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("rejects la médico." 0.1307 0.2677 0.7905 0.8070 0.8070 0.0165 0.00482 0.3280 0.0538 "UFSJES+Palatino-Italic" 0.0136 nil t 0)
  ("•  El/la miembro ‘member’ (of clubs), also el socio/la socia. The NGLE 2.9f approves of la" 0.1092 0.8471 0.8071 0.8235 0.8235 0.0165 0.00482 0.3764 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("miembro." 0.1305 0.2027 0.8236 0.8400 0.8400 0.0163 nil 0.3681 0.0722 "UFSJES+Palatino-Italic" 0.0136 nil t 0)
  ("•  El/la ministro ‘minister’, but la ministra is usual nowadays. El País and the Academy" 0.1092 0.8481 0.8400 0.8565 0.8565 0.0165 0.00482 0.3828 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("recommend la primera ministra over la primer ministro ‘prime minister’ although it logically" 0.1305 0.8970 0.8566 0.8730 0.8730 0.0165 0.00482 0.3844 0.1018 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("means ‘the first female minister’." 0.1305 0.4102 0.8730 0.8895 0.8895 0.0165 0.00482 0.3979 0.0552 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("•  La poeta is now preferred to la poetisa ‘poetess’." 0.1092 0.5337 0.8895 0.9060 0.9060 0.0165 0.00482 0.3258 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("•  La política is accepted by the NGLE 2.6g for a female politician; it also means ‘politics’. La" 0.1092 0.8975 0.9060 0.9225 0.9225 0.0165 0.00482 0.3588 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("informática is a female IT expert; it also means ‘computing’." 0.1305 0.6426 0.9225 0.9390 0.9390 0.0165 0.00482 0.3763 0.1017 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0))
  (18
  ("1.2  Group A: gender of nouns referring to human beings and to a few animals 5" 0.2845 0.9088 0.0492 0.0683 0.0683 0.0154 0.00456 0.3597 0.0274 "UFSJES+Palatino-Light" 0.0129 nil nil 1)
  ("•  La sacerdotisa is a possible feminine of el sacerdote ‘priest’, mainly used for ancient" 0.1092 0.8339 0.0832 0.0997 0.0997 0.0165 0.00482 0.3316 0.0165 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("religions. The NGLE notes the increasing use of la sacerdote for female (i.e. non-Roman" 0.1305 0.8586 0.0997 0.1162 0.1162 0.0165 0.00482 0.3414 0.0787 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("Catholic) priests." 0.1305 0.2742 0.1162 0.1326 0.1326 0.0165 0.00482 0.2989 0.0775 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("•  La sargenta is used to mean a bad-tempered, fierce woman, so la sargento is a female" 0.1092 0.8539 0.1327 0.1492 0.1492 0.0165 0.00482 0.3753 0.0166 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("sergeant." 0.1307 0.2080 0.1492 0.1656 0.1656 0.0165 nil 0.1719 0.0773 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("Other nouns ending in -o may be regular: el arquitecto/la arquitecta ‘architect’, el biólogo/la bióloga" 0.0920 0.9082 0.1779 0.1943 0.1943 0.0165 0.00482 0.2926 0.0495 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("‘biologist’, el catedrático/la catedrática ‘professor’ (European meaning), el filósofo/la filósofa ‘philoso-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.1944 0.2108 0.2108 0.0165 0.00482 0.3095 0.0892 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("pher’, el letrado/la letrada ‘counsel’/‘legal representative’, el sociólogo/la socióloga ‘sociologist’, etc." 0.0920 0.9080 0.2109 0.2273 0.2273 0.0165 0.00482 0.2679 0.0512 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("Nevertheless, forms like la arquitecto, la filósofo, la letrado may be preferred in Spain. La magistrada" 0.0920 0.9080 0.2273 0.2438 0.2438 0.0165 0.00482 0.3396 0.1156 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("‘judge’ (higher in rank than a British magistrate) is now usual." 0.0920 0.6204 0.2439 0.2603 0.2603 0.0165 0.00482 0.3495 0.0585 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("(1)  Feminine forms are often used, even in educated speech, when the woman is not listening:" 0.0920 0.9081 0.2725 0.2890 0.2890 0.0165 0.00482 0.3330 0.0273 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("¿qué tal te llevas con la nueva jefa? ‘how are you getting on with your new woman boss?’, but me" 0.0920 0.9079 0.2890 0.3055 0.3055 0.0165 0.00482 0.3465 0.0367 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("han dicho que usted es la jefe del departamento ‘they tell me that you are the head of the department’." 0.0920 0.9081 0.3055 0.3220 0.3220 0.0165 0.00482 0.3470 0.0289 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("1.2.8  Nouns referring to mixed groups of males and females" 0.0920 0.7909 0.3461 0.3669 0.3669 0.0208 0.00676 0.3653 0.0609 "UFSJES+Univers-Bold" 0.0172 t nil 1)
  ("With the rare exceptions noted at 1.2.1, the masculine plural refers either to males or to both" 0.0920 0.9080 0.3745 0.3910 0.3910 0.0165 0.00482 0.2910 0.0413 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("sexes, which confuses English-speakers. Mis hijos means ‘my sons’ or ‘my children’; mis hermanos" 0.0920 0.9081 0.3911 0.4075 0.4075 0.0165 0.00482 0.3817 0.0495 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("means ‘my brothers’ or ‘my brother(s) and sister(s)’. The answer to ¿tienes hermanos? might be" 0.0920 0.9082 0.4076 0.4240 0.4240 0.0165 0.00482 0.3226 0.0552 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("tengo dos hermanos y una hermana ‘I’ve got two brothers and one sister’. Likewise hoy vienen los" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4240 0.4405 0.4405 0.0165 0.00482 0.2786 0.0428 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("padres de los niños ‘the children’s parents are coming today’. ‘The children’s fathers are coming’" 0.0920 0.9080 0.4405 0.4570 0.4570 0.0165 0.00482 0.3013 0.0500 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("would have to be clarified by vienen los padres de los niños—los padres solos = ‘the fathers on their" 0.0920 0.9081 0.4570 0.4735 0.4735 0.0165 0.00482 0.3107 0.0556 "UFSJES+Palatino-Italic" 0.0136 nil t 0)
  ("own’. Further examples:" 0.0920 0.2990 0.4735 0.4900 0.4900 0.0165 0.00482 0.3668 0.0480 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("los alumnos students/male students" 0.1092 0.4099 0.5043 0.5208 0.5208 0.0165 0.00482 0.3280 0.0214 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("los ingleses the English/English men" 0.1092 0.4153 0.5208 0.5373 0.5373 0.0165 0.00482 0.3388 0.0214 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("los niños children/little boys" 0.1092 0.3486 0.5373 0.5538 0.5538 0.0165 0.00482 0.3015 0.0214 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("los perros dogs/male dogs" 0.1092 0.3283 0.5538 0.5703 0.5703 0.0165 0.00482 0.3182 0.0214 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("los primos cousins/male cousins" 0.5233 0.7935 0.5044 0.5208 0.5208 0.0165 0.00482 0.3353 0.0214 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("los profesores teachers/male teachers" 0.5233 0.8285 0.5209 0.5373 0.5373 0.0165 0.00482 0.2798 0.0214 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("los reyes the King and the Queen/kings/" 0.5233 0.8666 0.5373 0.5538 0.5538 0.0165 0.00482 0.3166 0.0214 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("    the kings and queens" 0.5233 0.7230 0.5538 0.5703 0.5703 0.0165 0.00482 0.3317 0.0048 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 2)
  ("(1)  Feminine nouns refer to females only, so one uses the masculine in sentences like no tengo más" 0.0920 0.9081 0.5847 0.6011 0.6011 0.0165 0.00482 0.3605 0.0273 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("amigos que mujeres ‘the only friends I have are women’ or todos los profesores son mujeres ‘all the" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6011 0.6176 0.6176 0.0165 0.00482 0.3097 0.0578 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("teachers are women’. ?No tengo más amigas que mujeres means ‘the only women friends I have are" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6177 0.6341 0.6341 0.0165 0.00482 0.3682 0.0699 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("women’! Tú eres la más inteligente de todos ‘you’re the most intelligent of all’ is a better compliment" 0.0920 0.9081 0.6341 0.6506 0.6506 0.0165 0.00482 0.4060 0.0747 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("to a woman than . . .de todas since the feminine excludes males. But a sentence like María es la" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6506 0.6671 0.6671 0.0165 0.00482 0.3368 0.0168 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("mejor profesora del instituto ‘Maria’s the best teacher in the school’ is ambiguous: it may or may not" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6671 0.6836 0.6836 0.0165 0.00482 0.3890 0.0439 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("include males. Emilia Pardo Bazán es la mejor intérprete de la vida rural de toda la literatura española" 0.0920 0.9080 0.6836 0.7001 0.7001 0.0163 0.00482 0.2949 0.0636 "UFSJES+Palatino-Italic" 0.0136 nil t 0)
  ("del siglo XIX ‘Emilia Pardo Bazán is the best interpreter of rural life in the whole of nineteenth-" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7001 0.7166 0.7166 0.0165 0.00482 0.3175 0.0225 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("century Spanish literature’ is assumed to mean that she is better than everybody. If ‘the best" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7166 0.7331 0.7331 0.0165 0.00482 0.2619 0.0652 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("female interpreter’ were meant one would say intérprete femenina." 0.0920 0.6467 0.7331 0.7496 0.7496 0.0165 0.00482 0.3573 0.0571 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("(2)  Care must be taken with words like uno, otro. If a woman from Madrid says todos los madrile-" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7618 0.7783 0.7783 0.0165 0.00482 0.3950 0.0273 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("ños me caen gordos ‘all Madrid people get on my nerves’ one could reply ¡pero tú eres uno de ellos!" 0.0920 0.9081 0.7783 0.7948 0.7948 0.0165 0.00482 0.3330 0.0267 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("‘but you’re one of them!’, but not * . . . una de ellos, since madrileños includes both males and" 0.0920 0.9080 0.7948 0.8113 0.8113 0.0165 0.00482 0.3023 0.0339 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("females (¡pero tú también eres madrileña! avoids the problem). Compare also Ana es una de las" 0.0920 0.9081 0.8113 0.8278 0.8278 0.0165 0.00482 0.2925 0.0653 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("profesoras ‘Ana is one of the women teachers’ and Ana es uno de los profesores ‘Ana is one of the" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8278 0.8443 0.8443 0.0165 0.00482 0.3077 0.0810 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("teachers’. In a few cases, usage seems uncertain. A woman might say either unos están a favor y" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8443 0.8608 0.8608 0.0165 0.00482 0.3226 0.0801 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("otros en contra. Yo soy de las que están a favor or . . . de los que están a favor ‘some are for, others are" 0.0920 0.9080 0.8608 0.8773 0.8773 0.0165 0.00482 0.2720 0.0382 "UFSJES+Palatino-Italic" 0.0136 nil t 0)
  ("against. I’m one of those who are for it’." 0.0920 0.4292 0.8773 0.8938 0.8938 0.0165 0.00482 0.3716 0.0660 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0)
  ("(3)  The fact that the masculine includes the feminine irritates some feminists since a phrase like" 0.0920 0.9081 0.9060 0.9225 0.9225 0.0165 0.00482 0.3463 0.0273 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 1)
  ("oportunidades para alumnos de química ‘opportunities for students of chemistry’ does not clearly" 0.0920 0.9080 0.9225 0.9390 0.9390 0.0165 0.00482 0.2743 0.1102 "UFSJES+Palatino-Roman" 0.0136 nil nil 0))
